1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,955
Dev says everyone will buy Laddoo Lab.

2
00:00:03,255 --> 00:00:05,365
Arjun says only parents will.

3
00:00:05,365 --> 00:00:06,505
Who is right?

4
00:00:07,055 --> 00:00:08,135
Nobody knows.

5
00:00:08,135 --> 00:00:10,793
Two strong opinions, and zero facts.

6
00:00:11,443 --> 00:00:13,349
So Zoya decides to find out.

7
00:00:13,349 --> 00:00:16,089
She takes a short survey to a school gate.

8
00:00:16,439 --> 00:00:19,762
Then to a gym, and to the
offices near her college.

9
00:00:20,112 --> 00:00:22,955
Five simple questions,
asked to real people.

10
00:00:23,605 --> 00:00:25,538
So what is marketing research?

11
00:00:25,838 --> 00:00:30,540
Kotler and Armstrong call it the
systematic design, collection, analysis

12
00:00:30,540 --> 00:00:34,650
and reporting of data, about a
specific marketing situation.

13
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:37,840
Systematic means planned, step by step.

14
00:00:37,840 --> 00:00:38,929
Not guessing.

15
00:00:39,279 --> 00:00:43,142
In simple words: ask the right people
the right question, in the right

16
00:00:43,142 --> 00:00:44,042
way.

17
00:00:44,227 --> 00:00:45,703
They give four steps.

18
00:00:45,703 --> 00:00:47,572
Let's watch Zoya follow them.

19
00:00:47,922 --> 00:00:48,822
Step one.

20
00:00:48,699 --> 00:00:50,008
Define the problem.

21
00:00:50,008 --> 00:00:52,539
Who will buy healthy laddoos, and why?

22
00:00:52,889 --> 00:00:53,789
Step two.

23
00:00:53,664 --> 00:00:54,922
Plan the research.

24
00:00:54,922 --> 00:00:57,826
Which questions, which
people, and how many?

25
00:00:58,176 --> 00:01:00,607
She picks a hundred and fifty people.

26
00:01:00,607 --> 00:01:04,524
Fifty at school gates, fifty
at gyms, and fifty in offices.

27
00:01:04,874 --> 00:01:05,853
Step three.

28
00:01:05,853 --> 00:01:07,375
Collect and analyze.

29
00:01:07,375 --> 00:01:10,547
Zoya and Arjun ask,
and count every answer.

30
00:01:10,897 --> 00:01:11,809
Step four.

31
00:01:11,809 --> 00:01:13,476
Report what you found.

32
00:01:13,826 --> 00:01:17,266
Most parents want a healthy
sweet for tiffin boxes.

33
00:01:17,266 --> 00:01:19,162
Gym-goers want more protein.

34
00:01:19,512 --> 00:01:22,872
And office workers want a
small snack at four o'clock.

35
00:01:23,222 --> 00:01:25,799
So Dev and Arjun were both half right.

36
00:01:25,799 --> 00:01:28,111
Now Zoya has facts, not opinions.

37
00:01:28,761 --> 00:01:30,602
Opinions start the argument.

38
00:01:30,602 --> 00:01:31,995
Research settles it.

39
00:01:32,695 --> 00:01:35,112
Research uses two kinds of data.

40
00:01:35,462 --> 00:01:37,488
Secondary data already exists.

41
00:01:37,488 --> 00:01:41,284
Reports, government numbers,
and your own sales records.

42
00:01:41,284 --> 00:01:42,963
It is cheap and quick.

43
00:01:43,313 --> 00:01:44,683
Primary data is new.

44
00:01:44,683 --> 00:01:47,605
You collect it yourself,
for your own question.

45
00:01:47,605 --> 00:01:49,498
It costs more, but it fits.

46
00:01:50,148 --> 00:01:53,167
There are three main ways
to collect primary data.

47
00:01:53,517 --> 00:01:54,424
Observation.

48
00:01:54,424 --> 00:01:55,959
Watch what people do.

49
00:01:55,959 --> 00:01:59,548
Arjun notes which snacks sell
out first at the canteen.

50
00:01:59,898 --> 00:02:00,798
Surveys.

51
00:02:00,594 --> 00:02:03,969
Ask people questions, like
Zoya at the school gate.

52
00:02:04,319 --> 00:02:05,219
Experiments.

53
00:02:05,193 --> 00:02:07,257
Change one thing, and compare.

54
00:02:07,257 --> 00:02:09,322
Same laddoo, two pack colours.

55
00:02:09,322 --> 00:02:10,551
Which sells more?

56
00:02:11,201 --> 00:02:13,168
Where do we see this at work today?

57
00:02:13,518 --> 00:02:14,780
Secondary data.

58
00:02:14,780 --> 00:02:18,836
A hospital uses public health
numbers to plan its beds.

59
00:02:19,186 --> 00:02:20,086
Surveys.

60
00:02:19,912 --> 00:02:23,327
An airline sends a short
survey after every flight.

61
00:02:23,677 --> 00:02:24,648
Observation.

62
00:02:24,648 --> 00:02:28,156
A bakery watches which
shelf people stop at first.

63
00:02:28,506 --> 00:02:29,406
Experiments.

64
00:02:29,391 --> 00:02:34,098
A food delivery app tests two button
colours to see which gets more orders.

65
00:02:34,748 --> 00:02:36,418
But can research go wrong?

66
00:02:36,768 --> 00:02:37,924
The good side.

67
00:02:37,924 --> 00:02:42,916
It replaces guesses with facts, and
stops bad ideas before they cost money.

68
00:02:43,266 --> 00:02:44,336
The hard side.

69
00:02:44,336 --> 00:02:45,991
It takes time and money.

70
00:02:45,991 --> 00:02:48,480
And a bad sample gives a wrong answer.

71
00:02:48,830 --> 00:02:53,141
If Zoya asked only her college
friends, she would think everyone loves

72
00:02:53,141 --> 00:02:54,041
laddoos.

73
00:02:54,192 --> 00:02:55,344
Friends are kind.

74
00:02:55,344 --> 00:02:57,108
They are not a fair sample.

75
00:02:57,458 --> 00:03:02,951
In 1936, an American magazine, the
Literary Digest, polled over two million

76
00:03:02,951 --> 00:03:06,564
people, and predicted the
wrong election winner.

77
00:03:06,914 --> 00:03:11,067
Its names came from phone books and
club lists, so it heard mostly from

78
00:03:11,067 --> 00:03:12,046
richer people.

79
00:03:12,046 --> 00:03:15,614
George Gallup asked about fifty
thousand, and got it right.

80
00:03:15,964 --> 00:03:16,864
The lesson?

81
00:03:16,778 --> 00:03:19,416
Ask the right people,
not just more people.

82
00:03:20,066 --> 00:03:21,020
Quick check.

83
00:03:21,020 --> 00:03:25,368
Zoya sells the same laddoo in two
pack colours, and compares sales.

84
00:03:25,368 --> 00:03:27,385
Which research method is this?

85
00:03:29,285 --> 00:03:30,294
An experiment!

86
00:03:30,294 --> 00:03:33,294
She changes one thing,
and compares the result.

87
00:03:33,894 --> 00:03:34,903
So, remember.

88
00:03:35,203 --> 00:03:39,160
Marketing research is a planned way
to collect facts for one marketing

89
00:03:39,160 --> 00:03:40,060
question.

90
00:03:40,214 --> 00:03:44,556
It has four steps: define, plan,
collect and analyze, then report.

91
00:03:44,906 --> 00:03:47,498
And a fair sample matters
more than a big one.

92
00:03:48,098 --> 00:03:51,086
Next time, we'll explore
market segmentation.
